WEBVTT

00:00:00.371 --> 00:00:04.786
20 September 19 hundred – 16 September 19

00:00:04.854 --> 00:00:06.195
69

00:00:07.087 --> 00:00:10.916
In 19 32 a typographer wrote fourteen

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:14.944
lines about what a printing office is for.

00:00:15.716 --> 00:00:18.151
They were set in Eric Gill's Perpetua

00:00:18.239 --> 00:00:21.803
Titling for a Monotype type specimen, and

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.579
they got away: reprinted more than

00:00:24.652 --> 00:00:27.802
possibly any other broadside in the trade,

00:00:27.866 --> 00:00:31.089
translated into seventeen languages, cast

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.494
in bronze in Washington.

00:00:34.279 --> 00:00:37.370
Ninety-four years later they describe this

00:00:37.431 --> 00:00:39.899
project better than anything we have

00:00:39.959 --> 00:00:42.095
written about ourselves.

00:00:42.895 --> 00:00:45.493
So we adapted them.

00:00:46.256 --> 00:00:48.966
This page shows you what she wrote, what

00:00:49.028 --> 00:00:51.644
we changed and who she was.

00:00:52.777 --> 00:00:54.323
What she wrote

00:00:55.291 --> 00:00:59.146
The 19 32 setting was capitals only,

00:00:59.207 --> 00:01:02.218
because the broadside existed to show off

00:01:02.255 --> 00:01:04.194
a titling face.

00:01:04.962 --> 00:01:07.502
Every mixed-case version you have ever

00:01:07.622 --> 00:01:11.169
seen is already somebody's adaptation.

00:01:11.942 --> 00:01:14.668
This is the reading best supported across

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:17.745
the sources, in the British spellings of

00:01:17.798 --> 00:01:21.144
the Surrey company that first printed it:

00:01:22.273 --> 00:01:23.342
Hear it

00:01:24.679 --> 00:01:26.831
What we made of it

00:01:28.148 --> 00:01:29.217
Hear it

00:01:30.194 --> 00:01:33.444
The words are Warde's; the reading is a 20

00:01:33.505 --> 00:01:36.898
26 Australian one, and the voice is a

00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:37.969
model.

00:01:38.768 --> 00:01:41.795
Lawrence Lessig said creativity always

00:01:41.878 --> 00:01:43.969
builds on the past.

00:01:44.726 --> 00:01:48.037
It does - but the debt runs both ways: the

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.022
future owes the present the plain truth of

00:01:51.088 --> 00:01:54.294
what it borrowed and how it was made.

00:01:55.093 --> 00:01:58.562
So we label the voice as generated now,

00:01:58.621 --> 00:02:01.412
while a machine still sounds like one,

00:02:01.471 --> 00:02:04.159
because a safeguard that only holds while

00:02:04.216 --> 00:02:07.569
the technology is crude is a postponement,

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.495
not a safeguard.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:13.578
The proof rides inside the file - the AI

00:02:13.651 --> 00:02:16.532
origin declared in its credential - and

00:02:16.594 --> 00:02:19.344
you can verify it yourself.

00:02:20.122 --> 00:02:22.917
Fixed in time, having been verified in

00:02:22.985 --> 00:02:24.019
proof.

00:02:24.794 --> 00:02:27.827
Three changes and one addition, and we

00:02:27.886 --> 00:02:30.291
would rather list them than let you find

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:31.194
them:

00:02:31.923 --> 00:02:35.007
"This is a Printing Office" becomes "Lolly

00:02:35.068 --> 00:02:38.058
is a Production Studio", in the first line

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:39.669
and the last.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:44.618
Her subject was a room with presses in it.

00:02:45.401 --> 00:02:50.021
Ours is a room that emits PNG, SVG, PDF,

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:51.919
video and audio.

00:02:52.698 --> 00:02:55.943
The claim is the same claim.

00:02:56.679 --> 00:03:00.065
"words may fly abroad" becomes "ideas may

00:03:00.156 --> 00:03:01.794
fly abroad."

00:03:02.603 --> 00:03:05.593
Lolly is not a text tool.

00:03:06.345 --> 00:03:09.708
"Not to perish on waves of sound" becomes

00:03:09.751 --> 00:03:12.994
"Not to perish on shores of change."

00:03:13.758 --> 00:03:16.741
Her contrast was writing against speech,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.645
the permanent against the spoken.

00:03:20.434 --> 00:03:22.907
Ours is the permanent against version

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:25.065
drift, dead links and lapsed

00:03:25.126 --> 00:03:26.719
subscriptions.

00:03:27.513 --> 00:03:30.118
We added one line.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.963
After "Incessant trumpet of trade": "that

00:03:34.035 --> 00:03:36.493
no impostor can sound."

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.203
Her boast was that the press heralds the

00:03:40.298 --> 00:03:41.493
trade.

00:03:42.284 --> 00:03:45.969
Ours is that the herald cannot be faked.

00:03:46.728 --> 00:03:50.186
It is the only line here that is not hers,

00:03:50.247 --> 00:03:52.619
and the place the adaptation stops

00:03:52.697 --> 00:03:55.659
borrowing and answers - because she beat

00:03:55.751 --> 00:03:58.161
an impostor problem by becoming an

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.210
impostor, and a Lolly file is a trumpet no

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.369
impostor can sound.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.813
Everything else is hers, including the

00:04:07.873 --> 00:04:10.835
line this whole page is about - and

00:04:10.901 --> 00:04:13.277
including the one that now reads like

00:04:13.338 --> 00:04:14.620
prophecy.

00:04:15.378 --> 00:04:18.886
"Not to vary with the writer's hand" meant

00:04:18.946 --> 00:04:22.298
a copyist corrupting a text as he wrote it

00:04:22.351 --> 00:04:25.086
out again, adding what he liked and

00:04:25.147 --> 00:04:28.005
dropping what he didn't until it had taken

00:04:28.064 --> 00:04:30.419
on a life of its own.

00:04:31.185 --> 00:04:34.152
The writer's hand today wears many gloves:

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.881
a model retraining on its own output until

00:04:36.924 --> 00:04:40.063
the copy forgets the original, a file that

00:04:40.145 --> 00:04:42.927
only opens in software no one sells any

00:04:42.986 --> 00:04:46.272
more, a link that rots, a re-upload that

00:04:46.385 --> 00:04:49.514
silently strips the credit, a subscription

00:04:49.568 --> 00:04:52.820
that lapses and takes your work hostage.

00:04:53.598 --> 00:04:56.249
Different hands, faster or slower, the

00:04:56.322 --> 00:04:57.993
same corruption.

00:04:58.815 --> 00:05:01.718
She met hers with a press.

00:05:02.499 --> 00:05:05.720
We meet ours with a file that stays fixed

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:08.919
and carries the proof of what it is.

00:05:10.055 --> 00:05:12.827
Fixed in time having been verified in

00:05:12.893 --> 00:05:13.713
proof

00:05:14.725 --> 00:05:17.582
Warde meant a proof pull: the sheet you

00:05:17.643 --> 00:05:19.628
take off the press and check before

00:05:19.689 --> 00:05:21.694
committing to the run.

00:05:22.468 --> 00:05:25.358
The word survived into our trade almost

00:05:25.451 --> 00:05:28.975
unchanged, then quietly acquired a second

00:05:29.027 --> 00:05:31.794
meaning she could not have intended.

00:05:32.557 --> 00:05:35.474
Every Lolly export carries its own record

00:05:35.565 --> 00:05:38.137
- a signed, machine-readable account of

00:05:38.211 --> 00:05:41.788
what a file is, what it was made from and

00:05:41.848 --> 00:05:43.770
what was done to it.

00:05:44.519 --> 00:05:47.633
Proof in her sense is the check before the

00:05:47.696 --> 00:05:48.694
run.

00:05:49.438 --> 00:05:52.434
Proof in ours is the check anyone can run

00:05:52.509 --> 00:05:55.466
afterwards, on the finished file, without

00:05:55.529 --> 00:05:57.044
asking us.

00:05:57.839 --> 00:06:00.714
Same word, same job, ninety-four years

00:06:00.774 --> 00:06:01.944
apart.

00:06:02.742 --> 00:06:05.868
You can verify it yourself.

00:06:07.009 --> 00:06:08.642
Who she was

00:06:09.666 --> 00:06:12.629
Beatrice Lamberton Warde, born Becker in

00:06:12.691 --> 00:06:16.140
New York on 20 September 19 hundred,

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.867
daughter of the Herald Tribune book critic

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.745
May Lamberton Becker and the composer

00:06:21.808 --> 00:06:23.494
Gustave Becker.

00:06:24.307 --> 00:06:27.167
She was shown calligraphy at thirteen,

00:06:27.229 --> 00:06:31.175
took her degree at Barnard in 19 21 and

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:34.370
went to work as assistant librarian at the

00:06:34.459 --> 00:06:37.295
Typographical Library and Museum of the

00:06:37.355 --> 00:06:40.048
American Type Founders Company in Jersey

00:06:40.137 --> 00:06:43.245
City, under Henry Lewis Bullen.

00:06:44.053 --> 00:06:47.769
She left for Europe in 19 25.

00:06:48.533 --> 00:06:51.816
In 19 26 The Fleuron published "The

00:06:51.882 --> 00:06:54.952
Garamond Types, Sixteenth and Seventeenth

00:06:55.013 --> 00:06:57.419
Century Sources Considered."

00:06:58.185 --> 00:07:01.050
The argument: the types held at the French

00:07:01.139 --> 00:07:04.027
royal printing office and sold across the

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:07.203
trade as Garamond's were not cut by Claude

00:07:07.263 --> 00:07:09.020
Garamond at all.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.099
They traced to Jean Jannon of Sedan, on

00:07:13.158 --> 00:07:16.872
the evidence of his 16 21 specimen sitting

00:07:16.914 --> 00:07:19.470
in the Bibliothèque Mazarine.

00:07:20.246 --> 00:07:23.380
The misattribution had entered print in 18

00:07:23.444 --> 00:07:27.769
45 and nobody had gone back to check.

00:07:28.538 --> 00:07:31.752
The author was named as Paul Beaujon, a

00:07:31.812 --> 00:07:34.920
man who did not exist, with an invented

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.570
biography to go with him.

00:07:38.382 --> 00:07:40.943
She was twenty-five.

00:07:41.726 --> 00:07:44.190
When Monotype offered Beaujon the

00:07:44.282 --> 00:07:47.460
editorship of The Monotype Recorder in 19

00:07:47.522 --> 00:07:51.706
27, the executives were expecting him to

00:07:51.787 --> 00:07:53.419
be a man.

00:07:54.214 --> 00:07:57.184
She took the job, became head of publicity

00:07:57.235 --> 00:08:01.820
in 19 29 and stayed until 19 60.

00:08:02.628 --> 00:08:05.318
She kept writing as him.

00:08:06.088 --> 00:08:08.610
The phrase every designer knows first

00:08:08.658 --> 00:08:12.361
appears under the Beaujon name in 19 33,

00:08:12.421 --> 00:08:15.358
in an essay called "On the Choice of Type

00:08:15.419 --> 00:08:19.189
Faces", and it is usually quoted with its

00:08:19.257 --> 00:08:21.169
first half cut off:

00:08:21.875 --> 00:08:25.055
"People who love ideas must have a love of

00:08:25.122 --> 00:08:26.244
words.

00:08:27.011 --> 00:08:29.659
They will take a vivid interest in the

00:08:29.747 --> 00:08:32.194
clothes that words wear."

00:08:32.916 --> 00:08:35.796
- Beatrice Warde, collected in The Crystal

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:38.595
Goblet, 19 55

00:08:39.594 --> 00:08:42.215
The half that gets dropped is the half

00:08:42.277 --> 00:08:44.894
that makes it an argument.

00:08:45.694 --> 00:08:48.754
Loving ideas obliges you to care about

00:08:48.824 --> 00:08:52.252
words, and caring about words obliges you

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:54.794
to care how they are dressed.

00:08:55.575 --> 00:08:58.723
Meaning is not changed by its setting, but

00:08:58.795 --> 00:09:01.990
its reception entirely is: the same

00:09:02.085 --> 00:09:04.674
sentence in the wrong face is a different

00:09:04.749 --> 00:09:07.744
sentence to the person reading it.

00:09:08.514 --> 00:09:11.773
That is the case for rigour at scale, made

00:09:11.805 --> 00:09:14.828
in two lines by a woman writing under a

00:09:14.889 --> 00:09:17.385
man's name because the trade would not

00:09:17.447 --> 00:09:19.019
print her own.

00:09:19.803 --> 00:09:22.195
So the woman who wrote "fixed in time

00:09:22.254 --> 00:09:25.541
having been verified in proof" is the same

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.544
person who went to the archive, found a

00:09:28.662 --> 00:09:30.989
three-hundred-year misattribution,

00:09:31.053 --> 00:09:33.267
published the correction under a false

00:09:33.326 --> 00:09:35.843
name because that was the price of being

00:09:35.916 --> 00:09:38.917
read and then had to claim it back as her

00:09:38.977 --> 00:09:39.970
own.

00:09:40.766 --> 00:09:44.241
She died in Epsom, Surrey, England on 16

00:09:44.303 --> 00:09:46.919
September 19 69.

00:09:48.047 --> 00:09:50.283
What we take from her

00:09:51.234 --> 00:09:52.559
Quality.

00:09:53.341 --> 00:09:56.267
Her argument in the crystal goblet essay

00:09:56.357 --> 00:09:58.318
is that the craft succeeds by

00:09:58.378 --> 00:10:01.451
disappearing: you should see the wine, not

00:10:01.504 --> 00:10:02.820
the glass.

00:10:03.581 --> 00:10:07.344
Constraint-first tooling is the same bet.

00:10:08.106 --> 00:10:11.144
The template holds the brand so the person

00:10:11.204 --> 00:10:13.904
using it can think about the message

00:10:14.024 --> 00:10:16.420
instead of the margins.

00:10:17.192 --> 00:10:18.693
Correctness.

00:10:19.497 --> 00:10:20.868
She checked.

00:10:21.638 --> 00:10:23.724
The whole trade had been calling a

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:26.371
typeface by the wrong name for three

00:10:26.451 --> 00:10:29.394
centuries because nobody had.

00:10:30.163 --> 00:10:32.722
Every claim on this site is paired with

00:10:32.784 --> 00:10:35.946
the mechanism that enforces it, and none

00:10:35.983 --> 00:10:38.719
of them ask for your trust.

00:10:39.502 --> 00:10:40.794
Fairness.

00:10:41.601 --> 00:10:44.616
She had to invent a man in order to be

00:10:44.687 --> 00:10:48.579
read - a false name and a false biography,

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:51.352
the whole apparatus of a person who did

00:10:51.396 --> 00:10:54.292
not exist, because that was the price of

00:10:54.352 --> 00:10:55.870
being printed.

00:10:56.643 --> 00:10:59.619
That is not a charming anecdote, it is

00:10:59.682 --> 00:11:01.994
what the field cost her.

00:11:02.763 --> 00:11:05.302
Her story is familiar to anyone who has

00:11:05.347 --> 00:11:08.428
had to mask who they are, or reinvent

00:11:08.472 --> 00:11:11.714
themselves, to be taken seriously in a

00:11:11.775 --> 00:11:14.616
room that was not built for them - which

00:11:14.679 --> 00:11:17.687
is why who gets to make things is a design

00:11:17.733 --> 00:11:20.770
decision here rather than a footnote.

00:11:21.549 --> 00:11:23.319
Attribution.

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:27.318
This is the one she is owed.

00:11:28.069 --> 00:11:31.388
Provenance is on by default here, and the

00:11:31.452 --> 00:11:35.273
chain covers ingredients as well as edits,

00:11:35.354 --> 00:11:37.879
so a work's history travels with it

00:11:37.944 --> 00:11:40.483
instead of being reconstructed by a

00:11:40.544 --> 00:11:42.270
stranger later.

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:46.224
We adapted her in public and listed our

00:11:46.289 --> 00:11:47.718
changes.

00:11:48.515 --> 00:11:51.543
Adapted from Beatrice L.

00:11:52.325 --> 00:11:55.460
Warde's "This is a Printing Office", first

00:11:55.521 --> 00:11:58.539
printed in 19 32 by the Monotype

00:11:58.601 --> 00:12:01.369
Corporation, Salfords, Surrey.

00:12:02.145 --> 00:12:04.504
Reproduced and adapted here with

00:12:04.561 --> 00:12:08.001
attribution, in the tradition of a text

00:12:08.081 --> 00:12:10.633
the printing trade has reprinted,

00:12:10.697 --> 00:12:13.430
translated and reset for ninety-four

00:12:13.497 --> 00:12:14.644
years.

00:12:15.427 --> 00:12:19.100
First printed in 19 32, not yet out of

00:12:19.162 --> 00:12:20.219
date.
